WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.489
Stars are just like us: nowhere near indestructible.

00:00:03.489 --> 00:00:08.540
While shooting a TV show can be a relatively
cushy job, it can also sometimes be dangerous,

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.830
and potentially even fatal.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.860
Here are some actors who were almost slain
due to stunts gone wrong, crew carelessness,

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.400
or their own near-fatal mistakes.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.440
Matthew Fox
While filming a scene for the final episode

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.880
of Lost in 2010, star Matthew Fox nearly lost
his life, according to co-star Jorge Garcia,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:28.119
who recounted the ordeal on his podcast about
the final season.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:32.570
In the show's finale, Fox's Jack and Terry
O'Quinn's Locke fight on a cliff, and at one

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:34.210
point, Locke stabs Jack.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.460
The show's creative team didn't think a collapsible
stunt knife looked convincing on camera, so

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.600
they filmed all the fighting with a real knife,
and planned to use the collapsible one only

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:43.830
to film the stabbing.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.129
But they forgot to swap out the knives when
it came time to shoot the scene, and during

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:50.780
one take, O'Quinn accidentally started to
stab Fox with the real deal.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.620
Fortunately, Fox took the stunt coordinator's

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:01.180
advice and wore protective kevlar under his
shirt, which likely saved his life.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:02.510
Mariska Hargitay

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.799
Actress Mariska Hargitay prides herself on
doing her own stunts on NBC's long-running

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:11.810
police procedural Law and Order: SVU, and
for about 10 years, Hargitay did so without

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.810
incident, until October 2008, when she "fell
wrong" while trying to execute a stunt.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.200
More accurately, she jumped, landed wrong,
and fell in such a way that she damaged a

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.369
lung, leading to a microscopic bleed.

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:27.010
Over the course of the next three months,
her lung slowly started collapsing, and she

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:31.049
eventually required multiple surgeries to
repair her lung and reverse the potentially

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.899
life-threatening condition.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:34.349
Kristin Chenoweth

00:01:34.349 --> 00:01:38.909
In the summer of 2012, actress Kristin Chenoweth
was filming scenes for what was supposed to

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:41.899
be a guest-starring arc on the CBS legal drama
The Good Wife.

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:46.560
But a freak accident ended those plans: a
piece of the lighting rig came loose and fell

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.679
right on her face, knocking her unconscious.

00:01:48.679 --> 00:01:53.909
"I was just standing there with my prop phone,
and I just remember saying, 'Oh, this phone

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:55.539
is …' That's all I remember."

00:01:55.539 --> 00:01:59.189
When she woke up later in the hospital, she
learned that she'd fractured her skull and

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:00.729
injured her hip and a rib.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:04.619
Two years later, she was still in physical
therapy to recover from the accident.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:06.169
Dominic Purcell

00:02:06.169 --> 00:02:11.330
In early 2016, Dominic Purcell was filming
scenes in Morocco for the revival of Prison

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.330
Break.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.510
In a bizarre turn of events, a piece of the
set broke free and fell right on top of the

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.579
actor's head.

00:02:16.579 --> 00:02:21.690
"Blood was just pouring all over me, and then
my stunt guy came up to me, and he says, 'Well,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.530
here's the thing: your head's cut open, I
can see your skull…"

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.150
Purcell suffered a broken nose and a minor
head injury.

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:32.730
But the show must go on: shooting continued
as Purcell was airlifted to a large hospital

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:33.939
in Casablanca.

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:38.099
Not long after the accident, Purcell reassured
fans on his Instagram page: "Pays to have

00:02:38.099 --> 00:02:41.219
a thick skull when an iron bar falls on your
head!"

00:02:41.219 --> 00:02:42.219
The Brady Bunch

00:02:42.219 --> 00:02:47.070
In 1973, The Brady Bunch filmed at the King's
Island amusement park in Mason, Ohio.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.829
In the episode, the plan was to have The Bradys
take a ride on the Red Racer roller coaster.

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:55.689
To get the shot, the producers wanted to put
a camera operator in the front car of the

00:02:55.689 --> 00:02:58.379
coaster to catch the actors' real reactions.

00:02:58.379 --> 00:03:02.829
Brady Bunch creator Sherwood Schwartz ordered
a test run of the coaster with just a camera

00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:06.280
mounted on the front car — no camera operator,
and no Bradys.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.019
The camera was too high and hit an overhang
during the test.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:12.829
Were it the real deal, the camera operator
would have been struck as well, and the ride

00:03:12.829 --> 00:03:15.140
could have been dangerous for him and the
Bradys.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.880
Ultimately, the camera was rigged in a lower,
safer, spot, and the scene was filmed without

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.469
a hitch.

00:03:20.469 --> 00:03:21.469
Aaron Paul

00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:25.389
In the DVD commentary for the first season
of Breaking Bad, Aaron Paul revealed that

00:03:25.389 --> 00:03:30.069
he was almost crushed while shooting a scene
in the desert in front of the show's RV-slash-mobile

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:34.510
meth lab. Paul adjusted his blocking while
filming with co-star Bryan Cranston, moving

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:38.670
a few steps away from where a massive rock
later fell from on top of the RV.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.189
Turns out, there was a tarp on top of the
RV held in place by the rock, and the rock

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:44.359
was dislodged by a strong gust of wind.

00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:49.190
As show creator Vince Gilligan notes, a 40-pound
rock falling 14 feet on top of Paul could

00:03:49.190 --> 00:03:51.540
have ended his life if it wasn't for the last-minute
adjustment.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.380
"Bam, I'm like, 'what the hell?'

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.380
y'know?"

00:03:54.849 --> 00:03:55.840
Thanks for watching!

00:03:55.849 --> 00:03:57.540
